WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.180
Heather du Plessis-Allan.

00:00:02.280 --> 00:00:05.955
So the question today is whether the
Taxpayers’ Union have gone too far with
their

00:00:05.955 --> 00:00:07.300
AI advert about Chris Hipkins.

00:00:07.800 --> 00:00:10.920
Now, if you haven't seen this ad yet, let me
explain it to you.

00:00:11.220 --> 00:00:16.542
The video starts, a short video, but it
starts with a man, a ginger-haired man in a

00:00:16.542 --> 00:00:17.760
suit and a red tie,

00:00:18.240 --> 00:00:20.700
running full tilt down a suburban street.

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:24.160
He kicks in a door. Inside the house is a
family having dinner.

00:00:24.260 --> 00:00:27.700
He runs up to them and he takes all their
wallets off the table and then runs off.

00:00:27.700 --> 00:00:31.420
And then he kicks in the door of another
house. A woman is watching telly in there.

00:00:31.500 --> 00:00:33.460
He runs up, takes her wallet, bolts it out
the door.

00:00:33.800 --> 00:00:37.480
Then he takes a wallet at a reno site and
then he takes all the money lying on a

00:00:37.480 --> 00:00:38.400
table at a barbecue.

00:00:38.860 --> 00:00:43.052
And then he runs out back onto the street to
a clapped-out red Toyota, gets in,

00:00:43.052 --> 00:00:45.440
looks in the rearview mirror and it's
Chippy.

00:00:46.020 --> 00:00:48.240
And then the ad says, We Can't Afford
Labour.

00:00:48.740 --> 00:00:53.357
Now, as you can imagine, commentators on the
left are absolutely losing their minds

00:00:53.357 --> 00:00:54.080
over this ad.

00:00:54.080 --> 00:00:58.702
Hipkins has called it nasty and desperate
and he says it's showing how out of touch

00:00:58.702 --> 00:01:01.320
the rich boys who run the Taxpayers’ Union
are.

00:01:01.500 --> 00:01:05.540
Frankly, I think it's brilliant. I think
it's a brilliant ad.

00:01:05.880 --> 00:01:10.200
I saw this ad weeks ago, funnily enough,
before the Taxpayers’ Union released it.

00:01:10.240 --> 00:01:12.620
I thought it was very, very funny the first
time I saw it.

00:01:12.940 --> 00:01:14.660
It doesn't cross ethical lines at all.

00:01:15.020 --> 00:01:17.140
No one will think it really is Hipkins.

00:01:17.140 --> 00:01:20.906
Not just because the AI actor doesn't
actually look like Hipkins, but because the

00:01:20.906 --> 00:01:22.720
behaviour is just so bloody outrageous.

00:01:23.280 --> 00:01:26.080
There's no way this is actually Chris
Hipkins doing it.

00:01:26.140 --> 00:01:28.780
You'd have to be an idiot to think that this
is real.

00:01:29.260 --> 00:01:32.560
The use of AI also is perfectly acceptable.

00:01:33.080 --> 00:01:37.019
And in fact, political parties would be
idiots not to use something this cheap and

00:01:37.019 --> 00:01:37.740
this effective.

00:01:38.160 --> 00:01:39.940
And that, I think, is the key thing here.

00:01:40.180 --> 00:01:43.980
This is the most effective attack ad on
Labour yet.

00:01:43.980 --> 00:01:49.311
This is a thousand times more effective than
every single one of the National MPs

00:01:49.311 --> 00:01:54.576
going “nine new taxes” on some kind of 1995
strategy in the hope that someone is

00:01:54.576 --> 00:01:57.340
going to take their false claim seriously.

00:01:57.740 --> 00:02:03.076
This ad takes an idea that Chippy is after
your money, which you know is true, and

00:02:03.076 --> 00:02:06.200
then exaggerates it to the point of being
funny.

00:02:06.560 --> 00:02:10.020
But because it's funny and because it's
visual, it sticks.

00:02:10.460 --> 00:02:12.380
Now, you might not like the Taxpayers’
Union.

00:02:12.380 --> 00:02:14.440
And you might not like their politics.

00:02:14.640 --> 00:02:15.900
You might not like their strategies.

00:02:16.260 --> 00:02:20.031
But you have got to admit that they are some
of the best in the business at

00:02:20.031 --> 00:02:23.500
campaigning and making a political point
with a little bit of humour.

00:02:23.860 --> 00:02:27.600
My advice to Chippy would be stop talking
about the ad, mate, because you are only

00:02:27.600 --> 00:02:31.431
drawing attention to something that
thousands of people are now going to want to
see

00:02:31.431 --> 00:02:33.940
and they're going to remember because it's
quite funny.

